--- body ---
## Context

The author, 2026-09-03, against the substrate problem this project keeps
hitting: **we cannot build on moving sand.** Zotero's structured-text pack is
the right substrate for the ratified unit of answer — `anchor.pageRects` per
block and a per-glyph `textMap` are entry-level location — but everything about
its *supply* moves. #6012 is a draft, `SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS` bumps
regenerate packs silently (`SYNC.md`), and a pack exists only for an attachment
opened in the reader: 2 of 13 631 on the author's machine.

His inversion: stop consuming an artifact whose producer moves, and **produce
the artifact ourselves, in their format**. The only coupling left is the
format. The consumer — segmenter, chunker, locator — is written once against
the pack and never changes on the day Zotero's packs arrive; it changes
feeder, not shape. And our producer is then genuinely throwable.

**Why this is cheaper than it sounds, and why it is not new work.** The
container writer already exists: `build_pack()` at
`tests/test_sdt_read.py:40` writes a valid pack byte by byte — header, index,
deflated metadata and page catalog, per-chunk offset tables — because the
malformed cases had to be constructed. The hard part was never the container
but the *blocks*, and those are the 0557 ladder we are already building (0560's
embedded TOC, 0561's layout heading heuristic on pdf.js text runs, 0562's
bylines). This ticket re-targets that ladder's output into the pack format
instead of an ad-hoc internal structure.

**Why it composes with them rather than fighting them.** `sdt.js` validates a
pack on `(packVersion, schemaMajorVersion, source.hash, processor.type,
processor.version)` and treats an unknown processor version as
`stale-processor`. So the degradation is already in our favour by their own
design: wherever their pack exists it wins, and ours is the placeholder for
where it does not.

## The ruling this rests on, and what is not settled

The author ruled the *shape* (option 1). The chain change it implies is **not**
ratified and goes to `DECISIONS.md` first, per the chain's rule: our packs are
a **third extractor identity** in C1 link 1, which today enumerates two —
Zotero's flat extraction and its pack. Ticket 0120's awaiting-ratification
entries of the same day carry the surrounding argument (R1 owes the quality
hole; supersession is total; the remedy is our own extraction) and this ticket
narrows *what* that extraction produces.

## What the re-cut of 2026-09-03 settles

The author asked whether the segmenter discussion has to be re-read now that
packs are to be available, and whether the flat-text segmenter is wasted work.
The answers decide this ticket's dependencies, so they are recorded here.

**seg/1 is not wasted, and it is not drain-only.** The ratified segmenter
interface takes the extracted text plus a list of structure signals, *each with
a provenance*, and returns entries carrying a title, a character range, an
optional page range, a confidence and the tier that cut them. Pack blocks are
**one provenance among six**. A pack reports that a block is a heading; it does
not report that this heading opens a new proceedings paper while that one is a
subsection of the previous. Validating a cut set against the document's own
contents list as a declared answer key, chapter and section numbering, the
confidence gate, the dictionary's median-gap rhythm — none of it is in a pack,
and none of it could be. The spec already rules seg/1 the implementation for
the empty signal list and the fall-through for every tier that comes up empty
or low, and those cases outlive universal packs: an attachment with no PDF, a
scanned book whose OCR layer yields no heading blocks, a single-work paper with
no internal structure, a pack whose heading set is degenerate. seg/1 is the
floor of the same function, not a rival path awaiting retirement.

**What packs replace is the signal-extraction half, and that is a
simplification.** The 0557 ladder welds two functions together: recovering
structure from a PDF, and cutting entries from signals. A pack does the first,
and does it better than a heuristic over pdf.js text runs. So under this
ticket 0561 stops being a segmenter tier and becomes a producer internal, and
on the PDF path the segmenter sees one structured input whoever produced it —
less machinery in the consumer, which is what condition 2 exists to defend.

**The outline does not follow it in.** The pack format has no outline field, so
0560 stays outside as its own provenance (see the log entry of the same day).
The seam between "inside the producer" and "outside it" is the schema's, not a
preference.

**What is proposed and not yet ruled:** this ticket now visibly holds two
units — the shape gate plus the writer, which depend on nothing, and the
producer that emits blocks, which needs 0560's vendored pdf.js for geometry.
Splitting them into two tickets is the author's call, not the agent's, and is
put to him rather than filed.

## Conditions, all of them the author's, and each one load-bearing

1. **"Compatible" means same container and same block schema, in OUR store.**
   Not byte-interoperable with Zotero's validator, not written into
   `storage/<KEY>/`. Correctness is owed to our own reader and to nothing else.
2. **Disposability discipline, and it is what makes or breaks the ticket.** No
   field of ours in the pack, no block type they do not have, no assumption
   that our packs are complete. One convenience field and the consumer has
   learned something about the producer, and the code stops being throwable.
3. **Only where Zotero has no pack.** Theirs wins wherever it exists, so the
   population we produce shrinks toward zero as they roll out: **the disposal
   is progressive, not a big-bang.**
4. **A cache, not a store.** Any pack may be deleted at any moment and
   re-derived; losing one costs CPU and never correctness. Written atomically
   `tmp` + rename, which is what `sdt.js` itself does.
5. **Self-stale, no parallel registry.** `source.hash` is their own field, so a
   pack whose file changed detects itself. Garbage collection is the item
   census the tick already runs for R1: key gone, pack gone.
6. **Never the source files.** Not the PDFs (read through the local API's
   `/file/view/url` redirect) and not `.zotero-ft-cache` (the API serves it
   byte-identical, measured, ticket 0483). Duplicating those would be
   re-inventing `storage/`.

## Actions

1. **Measure the size ratio first, before the shape is fixed.** The two real
   packs are on the reference machine and their byte sizes have never been
   read: `ls -l storage/GMAP993G/.zotero-sdt-cache` and `BSMCQ6BM`'s, each
   against its `.zotero-ft-cache`. The packs hold *less text* than the flat
   cache (0,91 and 0,99 measured, running heads excluded) but add block JSON,
   page rects and a per-glyph `textMap`, all deflated, and that last term is
   the one that could dominate. At a ratio near 1 over 8 590 PDFs this is of
   the order of a gigabyte beside a 1,71 GB index and the shape stands; **at a
   ratio near 3 this ticket is re-opened**, because keeping only the blocks the
   segmenter needs would break condition 2.
2. Promote `build_pack()` from test fixture to a real writer beside
   `verification/probes/sdt_read.py`, and pin it by round-tripping: every pack
   we write is read back by the existing reader and compared block for block.
3. Re-target the ladder's *layout* output into blocks and page anchors: block
   types drawn only from the taxonomy their bundle carries, page rects from
   pdf.js geometry, `source.hash` and a processor identity of ours in the
   metadata. This is the one action with a real prerequisite — 0560's
   vendored pdf.js, for the geometry — and it absorbs 0561. Actions 1 and 2
   above precede it and depend on nothing.
4. Source selection in the extract shim, one rule: their pack if present and of
   a known version, else ours if present and current, else the flat path. Never
   a direct read of `.zotero-ft-cache`.
5. **Plan the switch-over as a drain, not an event.** The day their pack
   replaces ours for an attachment, C1 link 1's identity flips, which is total
   supersession through all three links — a measured 8 h 14 for a full build.
   Newest-first, the older chain serving while it drains (R4, D3's
   serve-stale), and R17 saying where the drain is.
6. **Give our extractor the embedder registry's discipline** (tickets 0488,
   0495): a declared, dated, gated identity whose improvements are batched into
   rare bumps. Every improvement re-drains the library, so shipping them as
   they are written is not affordable.

## Test

- Round-trip: every pack the writer produces is read by
  `verification/probes/sdt_read.py` and refused by it when deliberately
  corrupted — reuse the existing malformed-case fixtures rather than inventing
  new ones.
- Red first, on the disposal property: a fixture attachment holding **both**
  our pack and a Zotero pack asserts the indexed text is Zotero's, and that
  removing our pack changes nothing at all. That test is what proves the
  consumer learned nothing about the producer.
- The two real packs stay the fidelity reference: our blocks over the same two
  documents are compared to theirs, and the comparison is reported, not scored.

## Verification

- [ ] The size ratio is measured before the shape is fixed
- [ ] A pack of ours round-trips through the existing reader, block for block
- [ ] With a Zotero pack present, ours is not read and its removal is a no-op
- [ ] No field, block type or assumption of ours appears anywhere in the format

## Invariants

- The entry as unit of answer is not reopened; this ticket serves it.
- Nothing is written into Zotero's data directory, and nothing is filed
  upstream. #6012 is read, not touched.
- The chain change (a third extractor identity in C1 link 1) reaches
  `DECISIONS.md` before any of it ships.
- No copyleft code vendored (MIT): the format is implemented from what we read,
  `pdf.js` is Apache-2.0 and is already vendored for tier 1 (ticket 0560).
- Coverage honesty survives: R17 counts pack-sourced-theirs,
  pack-sourced-ours and flat-sourced apart, and R24's locator says "page" only
  where a block anchor gives one.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The ruling is recorded in `DECISIONS.md` and `SPEC.md` C1 lists the third
      extractor identity
- [ ] The size ratio is in `bench/results/` and the shape decision cites it
- [ ] The writer ships beside the reader, round-tripped in `make check`
- [ ] The quality arm is run before any pack of ours is indexed: our blocks
      against theirs on the two real packs, and against X5's eleven uncapped
      attachments; a result worse than Zotero's current path stops the ticket
- [ ] The disposal path is written down: what is deleted, what drains, and what
      the user sees while it does
